<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Task D-3 (.superpowers/sdd/2026-08-02-dotD-modbus-rtu-blueprint/task-3-brief.md) — this assembly
         exists FOR ONE REASON: to be the only place in the product that depends on System.IO.Ports.

         net10.0-windows, not plain net10.0, because it ProjectReferences St4i.EdgeCore, which is
         net10.0-windows (DPAPI CredentialStore, a WPF-adjacent vendored SDK). System.IO.Ports itself is
         cross-platform; this TFM is inherited from what it sits on, not a statement about serial. -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <!-- N-1 — the compiler parses this project's `///` blocks and resolves every symbol they name. Set
         per-project on purpose: Directory.Build.props carries the scope, the measured price for all fifteen
         projects, and why the other nine are an owner decision rather than an implementer one. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\St4i.EdgeCore\St4i.EdgeCore.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- 🔴 Đợt D §6 — the FIRST NuGet exception in four batches, and it is deliberately bounded by this
         project's existence rather than by a promise in a comment.

         WHY THE PACKAGE IS ACCEPTED: published by Microsoft itself (not a third-party convenience
         library); it grants a capability that has no substitute (COM/RS-485 port access); and it is in NO
         shared framework at any TFM — verified on this machine rather than assumed:
         C:\Program Files\dotnet\shared\Microsoft.NETCore.App\{10.0.10,10.0.8,8.0.29} and
         Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App both contain no System.IO.Ports.dll, so it genuinely has to be
         restored.

         WHY IT IS IN A SEPARATE PROJECT rather than in St4i.EdgeCore beside the RTU framing layer it
         serves. NuGet copies a restored package's runtime assets into the output directory of EVERY
         project that transitively ProjectReferences it, whether or not that project's own IL touches a
         single type from it — the mechanism GĐ3 closeout WI-1 already had to unpick once for
         Makaretu.Dns (see St4i.EdgeCore.csproj's own comment, and MakaretuNotShippedTests). Putting the
         PackageReference on St4i.EdgeCore would therefore have put System.IO.Ports.dll into the build
         output of St4i.EdgeService, St4i.EngineApi and the WPF shell — i.e. into a gateway-only
         deployment, which the brief forbids. Scoping it here makes "the RTU framing layer and the gateway
         transport compile and run without it" a STRUCTURAL fact (they cannot see the types at all) rather
         than an inspection result; SerialDependencyScopingTests proves both halves, with a positive
         control so the absence assertions cannot pass vacuously.

         WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT BUY, stated so nobody over-reads it: a build that references this
         project ships the serial dependency, and one that does not, does not.

         🔴 TASK D-7c ANSWERED THAT CHOICE, and the answer inverts the paragraph that used to end this
         comment. D-3 recorded "today NOTHING in src/ references this project, so every shipping output is
         serial-free". That is no longer true and must not be left standing: on the owner's ruling of
         2026-08-03 all three hosts — St4i.EngineApi, St4i.EdgeService and St4iMachineSimulator — now
         ProjectReference this assembly, and all three therefore ship System.IO.Ports.dll. The three
         deployment assertions in SerialDependencyScopingTests were INVERTED to match (they now assert the
         dependency IS present) rather than deleted, so the capability cannot vanish silently in a later
         refactor.

         WHAT DID NOT CHANGE, and is the whole remaining point of this project: the PackageReference stays
         HERE and only here. St4i.EdgeCore — the RTU framing layer, the arbitration, the quarantine and the
         gateway transport — still compiles with no System.IO.Ports in sight, and
         SerialDependencyScopingTests.TheRtuFramingLayersOwnAssembly_ReferencesNeitherSystemIoPorts_NorTheSerialAssembly
         is untouched and still green. That assertion is what stops someone "simplifying" the layout by
         moving SerialPortBusLink back beside the seam it implements, which would push the package into every
         consumer of EdgeCore rather than into the three deliberate ones. -->

    <PackageReference Include="System.IO.Ports" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
